            <ArticleTitle>Gender differences in the use of peak flow meters and their effect on peak expiratory flow.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>526-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine if gender differences in the skill of using peak flow meters affect peak expiratory flow (PEF).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective observational study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University classroom.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred sixteen first-year pharmacy students (76 women, 40 men).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Students were taught correct use of a peak flow meter by means of classroom discussion and demonstrations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The students' technique in use of the peak flow meter was scored 3 times, and their PEF was recorded. Men scored higher than women (p=0.03) for the steps of &quot;inhale fully&quot; and &quot;exhale as hard and as fast as you can&quot; in the first attempt. Percentage increases in PEF did not significantly differ between the groups. Percentage change in PEF improved from the second attempt to the third attempt in women (p=0.036) but not men. On the third attempt, 13.2% of women versus 2.6% of men had an increase in PEF of more than 50% (p=0.1).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study found that men learned the correct technique for using a peak flow meter and attained their best PEF more quickly than women.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
